    <DataNode elementId="HYGROMYCIN-B" textLabel="hygromycin B" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="GRRNUXAQVGOGFE-GNNWKVJVSA-Q" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="chawkins, SRI, caspi">Hygromycin B is an aminocyclitol antibiotic produced by |FRAME: TAX-1912|. It is unusual in that it is active against both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells. It obstructs protein synthesis |CITS: [18567815]| by targeting 30S ribosomal subunits in bacteria |CITS: [11014183][12709313][17043113]| and the elongation of nascent chains during the translation of polysomes in eukaryotic cells |CITS: [367435][6433789]|. Hygromycin B was discovered in 1958 |CITS: [Mann58]|. Structurally it has a trisaccharide-like scaffold assembled from 2-deoxysteptamine (ring I), D-talose (ring II), and destomic acid (ring III). that are linked sequentially by glycoside bonds |CITS: [5498836]|. Rings II and III are linked by a rigidifying interglycosidic spirocyclic ortho-&amp;delta;-lactone (orthoester), making the compound a member of the orthosomycins. Hygromycin B has been produced on an industrial scale |CITS: [5298042]| and used to treat parasite infection in poultry and livestock |CITS: [14405260][14405258][4167359][5629634][5629663]| as well as in research, as a selection agent for genetic manipulation in biological studies |CITS: [6098829][24130756][24751825]|.</Comment>
